flview -- the FreeLFS image viewer ("Images")
=============================================

    flview [--rotate N] [--zoom PERCENT] [--no-strip] [FILE|FOLDER]

Opens a picture and keeps its whole folder at hand (PNG, JPEG, WebP; an
animated WebP shows its first frame). Without an argument it opens ~/Pictures,
else the current folder.

Keys
  Right, Page Down      next picture          Left, Page Up, Backspace  previous
  Home, End             first, last           + / - / wheel             zoom (wheel: at the pointer)
  0 or F                fit to the window     1                         100 %
  Up, Down              pan, or zoom when the whole picture is visible
  R / Shift+R, [ ]      rotate clockwise / counter-clockwise
  Delete                move the file to the Trash (~/.local/share/Trash, as flfiles does)
  W                     use it as the desktop wallpaper
  T                     show or hide the thumbnail strip
  Q, Escape             quit
Mouse: drag pans, double click toggles fit and 100 %, click a thumbnail to go
there, wheel over the strip steps through the folder. The toolbar has the same
actions as buttons (narrow windows drop the least used ones).

Wallpaper: the picture is copied to ~/.local/share/freelfs/wallpapers/NAME and
desktop.conf gets "wallpaper=THAT PATH" (fl_conf_set, the call the Settings app
uses for its keys); the copy means deleting the original is safe. The previous
copy is removed.

Decoding: libpng, libjpeg(-turbo) and libwebp directly (no gdk-pixbuf). JPEGs
are decoded at 1/2, 1/4 or 1/8 size when that is enough (thumbnails, and photos
over 24 megapixels), EXIF orientation is applied. Memory: the picture as ARGB
(4 bytes a pixel), one shrunken copy while zoomed out, up to 600 thumbnails of
at most 76 x 58 px; thumbnails are made one per timer tick, nearest the current
picture first, so the window never waits for them.

Rendering: bilinear (2x2 supersampled when shrinking a lot), nearest above
250 %; the finished view is cached, so moving the pointer does not redraw the
picture. Transparent pixels show a checkerboard.

Test: make check (python3; runs the viewer without a compositor). FLVIEW_KEYS=
"Right,w,Delete" feeds key presses into the first frame, for tests and
screenshots; FLUI_PPM=FILE writes the frame to a PPM (see libflui).
